by double digits, averaging around 11 points", "Strong Democratic fundamentals in Maine plus an open seat with no incumbent drag", "Pingree leads fundraising and has drawn bipartisan endorsements, including Sen. King", "Markets price the race near 95% Democratic"], "rationale": "Eight polls since late June all show Pingree up 9-14 points, and every major rater calls the race Solid or Likely D. Her fundraising edge, cross-party endorsements, and the Republican nominee's family-based controversies reinforce a clear Democratic advantage in a state leaning Democratic by about four points.", "watch": "A late independent candidacy by Rick Bennett siphoning moderate votes, or a shift in the affordability debate, could tighten the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Janet Mills (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Maine's governorship is open this year as Janet Mills is term-limited, and Democrat Hannah Pingree faces Republican Bobby Charles, with independent Rick Bennett also on the ballot. Polling has been remarkably consistent: every survey since late June shows Pingree ahead by nine to fourteen points, with the most recent live-caller polls near eleven. Expert raters uniformly place the race in the Solid or Likely Democratic column, and prediction markets price a Pingree win at roughly 95 percent.\n\nPingree has outraised Charles by about two to one and has emphasized bipartisan endorsements, including from Senator Angus King and some Republicans and independents. Charles has drawn negative attention from his own sister's public endorsement of his opponent. Affordability and housing dominate the campaign's issues, and national Democratic groups have scaled back spending out of confidence, leaving little evidence of a competitive race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9757, "margin": 10.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9716, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9705, "margin": 10.5}]}